+## 5. High — pausing traps user funds, and the trap can be made permanent
+
+```solidity
+function withdraw(uint256 shares) external whenNotPaused nonReentrant
+```
+
+`whenNotPaused` on `withdraw` means the pause switch is not a safety brake, it is a
+freeze on user funds. A pause intended to stop new deposits during an incident also
+stops everyone from getting out — exactly when they most want to.
+
+Worse, `Ownable.renounceOwnership()` is inherited and never overridden. Pause, then
+renounce, and no address in existence can ever unpause. Every depositor's USDT is
+locked in Aave forever.
+
+### Evidence
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-test 'test_F06_UsersCanExitWhilePaused'
+[FAIL: EnforcedPause()] test_F06_UsersCanExitWhilePaused() (gas: 2578471)
+```
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-test 'test_F06_PauseCannotBecomePermanent'
+[FAIL: OwnableUnauthorizedAccount(0x7c8999dC9a822c1f0Df42023113EDB4FDd543266)] test_F06_PauseCannotBecomePermanent() (gas: 2580605)
+```
+
+The second one: owner pauses, owner renounces, owner tries to unpause and is rejected
+as unauthorised. There is no other caller who could succeed.
+
+### Fix
+
+- Drop `whenNotPaused` from `withdraw()`. Pause deposits only. Exits should always be
+  possible.
+- If a withdrawal halt is genuinely required for some incident, make it a separate,
+  time-bounded flag that auto-expires, not the same switch as the deposit pause.
+- Override `renounceOwnership()` to revert, or use `Ownable2Step` with renounce
+  disabled.
+
